WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:35.260
This is a source-bounded teaching lesson on supraventricular tachycardia, or SVT, built only from the cardiology books and SVT screenshots in the local Cardiology Assistant library. We will move from mechanism and anatomy to ECG recognition, acute treatment, electrophysiology, and ablation. The aim is a cardiologist's mental model: identify the rhythm, identify the circuit, and then match the treatment to the circuit. This is an educational aid, not an independent bedside order set.

00:35.260 --> 01:23.320
Braunwald describes SVT as an umbrella term for tachycardias above the ventricle or involving tissue from the His bundle or above. The group includes inappropriate sinus tachycardia, atrial tachycardia, macroreentrant atrial tachycardia including typical flutter, junctional tachycardia, AV nodal reentrant tachycardia, and accessory-pathway mediated reentrant tachycardia. Paroxysmal SVT is the clinical subset with a rapid regular rhythm and abrupt onset and termination; that pattern is especially characteristic of AVNRT and AVRT. Grossman and Baim summarize the adult distribution as AVNRT, AVRT, and atrial tachycardia making up the great majority.

01:23.320 --> 02:09.475
The central mechanism is reentry. Topol describes three requirements: two functionally distinct pathways forming a circuit, unidirectional block in one pathway because refractory periods differ, and slow conduction down the available pathway long enough for the blocked pathway to recover. Park's pediatric text shows the same idea in AVRT and AVNRT: one limb conducts forward, the other returns the impulse, and the loop repeats. A premature atrial or ventricular beat can be the trigger when timing makes one limb refractory and the other excitable. The key teaching point is that the tachycardia is not simply a fast atrial focus; it is a self-sustaining circuit.

02:09.475 --> 02:48.850
For AVNRT, orient yourself in the triangle of Koch. The compact AV node sits at the apex. The region is bounded by the tendon of Todaro, the coronary sinus ostium, and the septal tricuspid annulus. Braunwald describes superior fast-pathway and inferior slow-pathway inputs, with additional left-sided inputs that can participate. The circuit may be right sided, left sided, simultaneous on both sides, or figure-of-eight. This is why ablation targets the slow-pathway region near the coronary sinus ostium rather than the compact His bundle itself.

02:48.850 --> 03:41.275
Typical AVNRT is slow-fast: antegrade conduction descends the slow pathway and retrograde activation returns through the fast pathway. The atria and ventricles are activated nearly together, so the P wave is hidden in the QRS or appears as a pseudo R-prime in V1 or a pseudo S wave in the inferior leads. The RP interval is short. Atypical AVNRT is fast-slow or slow-slow and usually produces a long-RP pattern with retrograde negative P waves in the inferior leads. During an electrophysiology study, a fixed VA relationship despite cycle-length variation favors AVNRT. Termination with a P wave as the final event also supports AVNRT rather than focal atrial tachycardia.

03:41.275 --> 04:46.395
AVRT uses an accessory pathway as one limb and the normal AV conduction system as the other. Orthodromic AVRT conducts antegrade through the AV node and His-Purkinje system, so the rhythm is usually narrow; the retrograde P wave often deforms the ST segment or T wave. Antidromic AVRT conducts forward through the accessory pathway and is wide and fully preexcited. In sinus rhythm, a manifest pathway produces the WPW pattern: a short PR interval, a delta wave, and a widened QRS. A concealed pathway may conduct only retrograde and leave the sinus ECG without preexcitation. The dangerous pattern is preexcited atrial fibrillation: an irregularly irregular wide-complex rhythm with very short R-R intervals. Braunwald identifies a shortest preexcited R-R interval below 250 milliseconds as a risk marker for ventricular fibrillation and sudden death.

04:46.395 --> 05:38.220
Focal atrial tachycardia arises from an atrial focus and often has a P-wave morphology different from sinus rhythm. Braunwald describes rates commonly between 130 and 250 beats per minute, with possible warm-up and cool-down behavior. Adenosine may terminate it, transiently slow it, or simply unmask the atrial activity with AV block. Chronic symptomatic focal atrial tachycardia is approached with mapping and ablation of the earliest atrial activation. Macroreentrant atrial tachycardia, including typical flutter, is different: a wavefront circulates around an anatomic or scar-related obstacle. Typical flutter uses the cavotricuspid isthmus and produces the familiar continuous saw-tooth pattern in the inferior leads.

05:38.220 --> 06:32.510
Start with a 12-lead ECG and ask three questions. First: are atrial and ventricular activations linked one-to-one? Second: is the RP interval short or long? Third: is the RP relationship fixed or variable? In typical AVNRT the P wave is inside or immediately after the QRS, creating a short RP. In AVRT the retrograde P wave is usually visible after the QRS, often in the ST segment. In focal atrial tachycardia the RP relationship is incidental and may vary or become unlinked. Chou emphasizes that vagal stimulation may terminate AVNRT or AV reentry but usually only causes transient AV block in focal atrial tachycardia and flutter; that response is a diagnostic clue, not just a treatment step.

06:32.510 --> 07:32.815
A supraventricular rhythm may become wide because of bundle-branch aberrancy or antegrade conduction over an accessory pathway, but ventricular tachycardia remains a major competing diagnosis. Braunwald's comparison table uses onset, the relation of P waves to QRS complexes, QRS duration and morphology, axis, concordance, and the initial QRS velocity. AV dissociation, capture or fusion beats, a very broad QRS, and an axis far outside the usual range favor ventricular tachycardia. A regular wide rhythm may be antidromic AVRT or SVT with aberrancy; an irregularly irregular wide rhythm with preexcitation points toward preexcited atrial fibrillation. If the mechanism remains uncertain, the books support structured electrophysiologic assessment rather than assuming that every wide rhythm is ordinary SVT.

07:32.815 --> 08:22.720
The first investigation is documentation. Obtain a 12-lead ECG in sinus rhythm and during the tachycardia whenever possible. Braunwald recommends routine biochemistry and thyroid function testing, and echocardiography to assess ventricular function, atrial size, and important valve disease. Match the monitor to symptom frequency: a 24-hour Holter when events occur daily, longer ambulatory monitoring for less frequent episodes, and an implantable loop recorder when simpler strategies fail. Kern makes the electrophysiology point explicit: surface ECG features may suggest the mechanism, but a detailed EPS is the accurate way to characterize the circuit and define the anatomic substrate.

08:22.720 --> 09:08.350
For a stable regular narrow-complex SVT in the book-based pathway, begin with vagal maneuvers in the supine position with leg elevation. If that fails, Braunwald and Hurst describe rapid intravenous adenosine: 6 milligrams followed by 12 milligrams if needed. Warn the patient about transient chest tightness, dyspnea, or a sense of impending doom. If the rhythm persists, intravenous verapamil or diltiazem, or an intravenous beta blocker, may be considered in a hemodynamically stable patient. Synchronized cardioversion is appropriate when the patient is unstable, when pharmacologic treatment is ineffective, or when medications are contraindicated.

09:08.350 --> 09:54.705
Do not carry the narrow-complex algorithm into every wide or preexcited rhythm. Braunwald states that an unstable preexcited atrial fibrillation patient should undergo direct-current cardioversion. If stable, drugs acting on the accessory pathway, such as ibutilide, procainamide, or a class 1C agent, are described. Braunwald warns that amiodarone and AV-nodal blocking drugs may accelerate ventricular rates and increase the risk of ventricular fibrillation in preexcited AF. For a regular narrow AVRT, adenosine can be effective, but Braunwald emphasizes caution because atrial fibrillation may be induced and electrical cardioversion must be available.

09:54.705 --> 10:46.455
Long-term treatment is individualized to symptom burden, episode frequency, predictability, occupation, and patient preference. Braunwald allows observation for infrequent mild episodes that terminate with vagal maneuvers. Recurrent, unpredictable, or severe symptomatic SVT moves the balance toward catheter ablation. For AVNRT, slow-pathway ablation is the treatment of choice, with reported long-term success above 95 percent in Braunwald. For symptomatic focal atrial tachycardia, ablation is presented as first-line therapy. For recurrent AVRT or sustained preexcited AF, accessory-pathway ablation is the treatment of choice, with contemporary success around 95 percent in the cited chapter.

10:46.455 --> 11:38.680
In the EP laboratory, pacing and intracardiac recordings define the activation sequence and reproduce the tachycardia. For AVNRT, ablation is directed to the slow-pathway region near the coronary sinus ostium. Grossman and Baim emphasize that noninducibility is the important procedural endpoint and that residual slow-pathway conduction or a single echo beat does not necessarily require complete elimination. Junctional rhythm can signal an effective slow-pathway site, but loss of VA or AV conduction during energy delivery is a warning to stop. The major procedural concern is inadvertent AV block. Long sheaths improve stability; cryoablation is an option when reversibility near the AV node is especially valuable.

11:38.680 --> 12:26.635
Checkpoint one: a sudden regular narrow rhythm with a P wave buried in the QRS, pseudo R-prime in V1, or pseudo S waves inferiorly is a classic AVNRT pattern. Checkpoint two: a regular narrow rhythm with a retrograde P wave deforming the T wave, especially with a known accessory pathway, points toward orthodromic AVRT. Checkpoint three: an irregularly irregular wide rhythm with preexcitation and very short R-R intervals is the dangerous preexcited AF pattern. The treatment branch changes at that point. The safest interpretation is not just a label; it is a mechanism-linked ECG diagnosis that explains why the next treatment step is appropriate.

12:26.635 --> 13:04.095
The practical checklist is short. Document the rhythm. Decide narrow or wide and regular or irregular. Find the P wave and measure the RP relationship. Ask whether AV nodal conduction is part of the circuit. Use vagal maneuvers and adenosine for the appropriate stable narrow rhythms. Treat preexcited AF as a different emergency pathway. For recurrent symptomatic disease, refer for EPS and discuss ablation. Every claim in this lesson is tied to the local textbook pages shown on screen and listed in the final references.

13:04.095 --> 13:50.030
Primary visual and teaching sources used are Braunwald pages 1350, 1351, 1353 through 1357, 1365 through 1375, and 1384; Hurst pages 2008 through 2014; Chou pages 399 and 403 through 405; Topol-Griffin pages 249, 255 through 258, and 261; Grossman and Baim pages 2150 and 2153; Kern page 495; and Park pages 315 and 316. The page numbers shown are PDF page numbers, with printed page numbers included where visible. No outside websites, guidelines, or media are used in this lesson.
